   "content": [
      {
         "id": "23595322-a40a-4c7a-b197-9782cac75c30",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Introduction\n\nWe often split things into two sides to understand them better: a piece of cake, a sheet of paper, or even our own body. This helps us see how things are made and how they work.\n\nNumbers can be split into two groups too. In this class, we will see how.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "8a0686bc-ba48-4f5d-aa25-6c59e7042a5f",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "8a0686bc-ba48-4f5d-aa25-6c59e7042a5f",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Perfect Division\n\nLook at the picture below. There are two light blue boxes, and each box has two rectangles.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div><table style=\"margin:0 auto;border-collapse:collapse;\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\" style=\"border:1px solid #333333;padding:6px 12px;font-weight:bold;\">Box Divided into 2</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height:100px;width:120px;background-color:#ADD8E6;border:1px solid #333333;text-align:center;\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span></td><td style=\"height:100px;width:120px;background-color:#ADD8E6;border:1px solid #333333;text-align:center;\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span></td></tr></table><p style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;font-style:italic;margin:6px 0;\"><b>Table 1</b>: Two boxes, each containing two rectangles.</p></div>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Each side received the same number of rectangles: two and two. Nothing is left over. When this happens, we say the division is exact.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "13366d45-51df-4ae8-9b1a-5da0705ca9b3",
         "prev": "23595322-a40a-4c7a-b197-9782cac75c30",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "13366d45-51df-4ae8-9b1a-5da0705ca9b3",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Remaining\n\nNow imagine that, instead of splitting 4 rectangles, we need to split 5 rectangles into the same two boxes.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div><table style=\"margin:0 auto;border-collapse:collapse;\"><tr><th colspan=\"3\" style=\"border:1px solid #333333;padding:6px 12px;font-weight:bold;\">Five Rectangles Shared into 2 Boxes</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height:100px;width:120px;background-color:#ADD8E6;border:1px solid #333333;text-align:center;\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span></td><td style=\"height:100px;width:120px;background-color:#ADD8E6;border:1px solid #333333;text-align:center;\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span></td><td style=\"height:100px;width:120px;border:none;text-align:center;\"><div style=\"font-size:11px;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:4px;\">Remaining</div><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:18px;height:36px;border:2px solid #333333;border-radius:3px;margin:0 4px;\"></span></td></tr></table><p style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;font-style:italic;margin:6px 0;\"><b>Table 2</b>: Five rectangles shared into two boxes. One rectangle is left over.</p></div>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Each box still receives two rectangles, but one rectangle remains outside. It cannot be placed in either box if we want the two groups to stay equal. We call it the **remainder**.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4dcbb6d3-104b-4e8c-9f16-a41baf2271df",
         "prev": "8a0686bc-ba48-4f5d-aa25-6c59e7042a5f",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4dcbb6d3-104b-4e8c-9f16-a41baf2271df",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Division by 2\n\nThis is exactly what happens when we divide, or share, by 2. If a number can be shared equally between two boxes, there is no remainder. We say the number is **divisible by 2**.\n\nIf a number cannot be shared equally, one object is left over. Then the remainder is 1.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "93809d37-e26b-4926-bd1f-4465149febfd",
         "prev": "13366d45-51df-4ae8-9b1a-5da0705ca9b3",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "93809d37-e26b-4926-bd1f-4465149febfd",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Thinking\n\nCan we find out whether a number is divisible by 2 just by looking at its remainder?\n\nChoose your answer below.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "fe46d728-c7f8-44ee-891d-482142c45658",
         "prev": "4dcbb6d3-104b-4e8c-9f16-a41baf2271df",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "yes",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "fe46d728-c7f8-44ee-891d-482142c45658",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Odd or Even\n\nWhen a number is divisible by another number, the remainder is zero.\n\nFor division by 2:\n\n- **Even numbers** end in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8. They can be shared equally between two groups, so the remainder is 0. Examples: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10...\n- **Odd numbers** end in 1, 3, 5, 7 or 9. When we divide them by 2, there is always 1 left over. Examples: 3, 5, 7, 9, 11... The number 1 is a special case that we will study in another class.\n\nSo the last digit tells us the group: if it is even, the number is even; if it is odd, the number is odd.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "0c74a998-5bc7-4130-ac47-bdada1318a43",
         "prev": "93809d37-e26b-4926-bd1f-4465149febfd",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "0c74a998-5bc7-4130-ac47-bdada1318a43",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned that numbers can be grouped into two groups: **even** and **odd**.\n\nWe also learned that every even number is divisible by 2, and every odd number leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 2. The last digit tells us which group a number belongs to.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "",
         "prev": "fe46d728-c7f8-44ee-891d-482142c45658",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      }
   ]
}
